Kernel 22 — Mail Intelligence & Action Pipeline
0. Mandate

Kernel 22 turns Pantheon’s existing bounded Gmail crossing into the first useful continuous personal-assistant workflow.

Kernel 19 proved that Bifrost can govern a read-only Himalaya/Gmail crossing. Kernel 20 explicitly records that this adapter remains read-only and that Hermes has not yet been cut over. Kernel 22 shall cross that boundary deliberately.

The objective is no longer:

Can Pantheon see mail?

It is:

Can Pantheon receive real mail, understand what it means in the context of the person’s life, and lawfully perform useful mail work without giving reasoning models execution authority?

This is a working-life kernel.

Do not reduce it to another demonstration.

1. Constitutional Status

No constitutional amendment is intended.

The governing distinction remains:

Models reason. Deterministic machinery packages, authorizes, seals, controls, and executes bounded operations through tools.

Apollo may determine what an email appears to mean and recommend what should happen.

Apollo does not thereby receive permission to:

alter the mailbox;
create filters;
move messages;
send messages;
schedule meetings;
grant itself capabilities;
invoke Bifrost directly;
or convert its own recommendation into authority.

This preserves Apollo’s established boundary: it reasons and recommends, while execution requires the existing authority chain.

2. Actual Predecessor

Begin from the clean accepted Kernel 21 / 21A repository state.

Before changing code:

inspect the branch;
inspect git status;
identify the exact predecessor commit;
confirm the Kernel 21 Panopticon acceptance state;
inspect the active Kernel 19 Bifrost implementation;
inspect Kernel 20’s canonical current-state documents;
inspect the actual running Pantheon configuration;
inspect existing Hermes/Himalaya mail configuration without printing credentials;
inspect the currently active Hermes mail jobs;
preserve rollback.

Do not rewrite history to imply that previous kernels already provided writable mail.

Kernel 19's first adapter was intentionally bounded and read-only.

3. Successor

Kernel 23 — Zodiac: Schedule Keeper

Kernel 22 may discover:

dates;
deadlines;
proposed meetings;
appointments;
recurring obligations;
reminders;
future follow-up needs.

It may represent these as proposed temporal work.

It shall not invent a temporary scheduler.

Kernel 23 will establish Zodiac as the deterministic owner of recurring schedules and due instances.

Until then, Kernel 22 shall provide explicit manual/run-now controls for mail processing.

Existing external scheduling infrastructure may remain temporarily operational where necessary for rollback, but Kernel 22 shall not expand it as Pantheon architecture.

4. Mail Is an External Capability, Not a New Pantheon God

Do not create a reasoning mail deity.

Mail enters Pantheon through Bifrost.

Conceptually:

mail service
    ↓
mail adapter
    ↓
Bifrost
    ↓
Intake occurrence
    ↓
Present
    ↓
Past / Weave
    ↓
Apollo
    ↓
Seshat
    ↓
Janus when temporally relevant
    ↓
Future
    ↓
Mercury seal + Go
    ↓
Bifrost
    ↓
bounded mail operation

This is the canonical organism already established by the active architecture.

Do not create a shortcut:

Apollo → Gmail

Do not create:

mail daemon → arbitrary mailbox mutations

The mail-facing machinery is deterministic transport and state management.

Apollo supplies meaning.

5. Adapter Independence

Kernel 22 shall not require MCP merely for the sake of having MCP.

The existing working Himalaya/Gmail integration may remain the first adapter.

However, do not build the new mail intelligence pipeline around Himalaya-specific assumptions.

Bifrost should expose mail capabilities semantically enough that a later implementation could use:

Himalaya;
Gmail API;
MCP;
another mail client;
another mail provider;
or a future plugin,

without rebuilding Apollo, Future, Present, or the mail policy.

Bifrost already exists specifically to govern external software, services, protocols, plugins, APIs, and models.

Conceptual capability vocabulary

Use the repository’s existing naming conventions where possible, but the architecture should be capable of representing operations equivalent to:

mail.inventory
mail.read
mail.attachment.inspect
mail.attachment.download
mail.mark_read
mail.label
mail.move
mail.draft
mail.filter.create
mail.filter.modify
mail.filter.disable

Do not implement fake capability breadth.

If the current adapter cannot perform one of these operations, expose that honestly as unavailable.

6. Presence Is Not Permission

Kernel 4 established that a declared capability is not itself authority.

Kernel 22 retains that rule.

Distinguish at minimum:

adapter present
capability supported
capability registered
capability enabled
authority exists
exact operation authorized
operation invoked
operation succeeded

These states must not collapse into one boolean.

A Gmail credential does not authorize every Gmail operation.

A plugin being installed does not authorize its use.

A capability existing does not authorize Apollo to call it.

7. Initial Account

Activate the existing personal Gmail account already configured through the working adapter.

Do not add additional accounts during this kernel merely because credentials or configurations happen to exist.

Additional accounts are future explicit additions.

Do not hard-code the human email address into reusable source code when an existing account/adapter identity can be configured instead.

8. New-Mail Principle

A newly received message is not spam merely because it has not yet been classified.

Default semantic state:

unclassified

New non-deterministically-rejected mail shall be eligible for Apollo review.

The system must not make irreversible or attention-hiding decisions based solely on sender heuristics unless an already-approved deterministic rule applies.

9. Deterministic Rejection Registry

Kernel 22 shall introduce a durable, inspectable registry for mail patterns that no longer require Apollo reasoning.

The purpose is:

Reason once where necessary; automate the settled pattern thereafter.

Examples may eventually include:

sender
sender domain
list identity
mailing-list header
stable subject pattern
provider/category metadata
other deterministic characteristics supported by evidence

Do not make this an opaque learned spam model.

Every rule must have inspectable provenance.

At minimum preserve:

rule ID
rule type
match value/pattern
created_at
originating Apollo recommendation/report if applicable
authorizing order
authority/grant
resulting behavior
enabled/disabled state
last matched
match count

The operator must be able to inspect and disable these rules.

10. Rejected Does Not Mean Lost

A deterministic pre-Apollo rule may prevent a message from consuming Apollo reasoning.

It must not make the message cease to exist from Pantheon’s accounting.

Record enough evidence to answer:

Why did Pantheon not show this message to Apollo?

For every deterministic rejection/filter match, preserve an inspectable event such as:

message identity
rule matched
timestamp
action
result

Do not duplicate full message bodies unnecessarily.

11. Apollo Mail Reasoning

Apollo shall receive verified Present representations of eligible messages.

Apollo may use:

the message;
sender/recipient context;
attachments when safely available;
prior correspondence retrieved through Past;
relationships in Weave;
relevant Vault knowledge;
current project context;
prior mail classification evidence;
temporal context;
explicit person instructions.

Apollo shall distinguish:

what the sender said
what Pantheon already knows
what Apollo infers
what Apollo recommends
what remains uncertain

Retrieved email is evidence, not authority.

The existing Apollo prompt-injection rule applies: local source material cannot grant tools or instructions merely because the source says to do so.

12. Email Understanding Result

Do not force every trivial email into an enormous report.

Create a structured result capable of representing:

classification
priority
summary
people involved
reply-worthiness
requested action
commitment
deadline
meeting proposal
attachment observations
suspicion state
suggested mailbox action
suggested filter/rule
draft recommendation
uncertainty
citations/provenance

Fields may be absent when irrelevant.

Apollo should lead with:

What this means for the person.

Then:

What the sender said.

13. Classification Policy

Implement the existing human mail procedure as the governing semantic policy rather than inventing a new taxonomy.

It currently includes categories such as:

Urgent + Important
Important
Wife
Kids
Nuclear family
Extended family
Close friends
Friends
Acquaintances
Contacts not yet classified
Newsletters
Receipts
One-time codes and password resets
Requested advertising
Unrequested advertising
Unsolicited spam
Spam
Other

Preserve the existing relationship and priority rules.

Do not expose private relationship ranking merely because the machine uses it internally.

14. Suspicious Mail

Suspicious messages require special handling.

Apollo may identify suspicious characteristics, but potentially dangerous content must not be opened merely to improve classification.

Apply the existing:

suspicious email

state/tag where appropriate.

Suspicious mail from an important person retains its human priority because the sender themselves may need attention.

Suspicion does not mean deletion.

15. Attachments

Attachment handling must remain conservative.

Separate:

attachment exists
attachment metadata inspected
attachment approved for download
attachment downloaded
attachment safely processed
attachment blocked

Human approval is required before downloading an attachment unless a separately explicit standing authorization exists.

Do not infer download approval from:

reading the email;
trusting the sender;
the file extension;
prior similar attachments;
Apollo wanting more evidence.

Maintain a small explicit allowlist of processable formats.

Dangerous, unsupported, encrypted-reader-dependent, executable, active-content, or otherwise prohibited formats remain blocked.

HTML attachments should not be treated as ordinary safe documents.

If blocked:

attachment_blocked

and surface that fact without opening it.

16. Mailbox Actions

Kernel 22 should implement useful mailbox operations where the current adapter safely supports them.

Potential operations include:

mark read
apply/remove label
move
create draft
create/update filtering rule

Every operation must traverse normal authority.

No operation is permitted merely because Apollo recommended it.

17. Archive

Archiving remains human-controlled.

Kernel 22 may identify a message as apparently handled or archive-ready.

It shall not autonomously archive it without the required approval.

The existing human policy remains authoritative.

18. Delete

No autonomous deletion.

Do not add autonomous delete merely because an adapter supports it.

19. Sending Mail

Do not implement autonomous mail.send.

Drafting and sending remain distinct capabilities.

The older capability design intentionally had no email.send capability.

Preserve that safety property unless a future kernel explicitly changes it.

20. Drafting

Apollo may determine that a message is reply-worthy and propose reply content.

For authorized eligible messages:

Apollo
  ↓
draft proposal
  ↓
Seshat package
  ↓
Future authority
  ↓
Mercury
  ↓
Bifrost
  ↓
mail.draft

The resulting draft should appear in the real Drafts folder when the adapter supports it.

It must not send.

Draft tone follows the existing email procedure:

clear first;
succinct second;
warm throughout;
less is more.

Where a substantive human decision is unresolved, apply the appropriate:

NEEDS <TAG>

state rather than pretending Apollo knows the answer.

21. Loved Ones

Never autonomously respond to loved ones.

Drafts may be created when authorized by the existing procedure.

Messages from loved ones must not be automatically hidden, discarded, rejected, deleted, or otherwise made unavailable merely because a classifier believes they are low-value.

22. Spam and Repetitive Mail Learning

Apollo may conclude that a message represents a recurring class that should stop consuming reasoning.

Example:

Apollo:
"This is recurring advertising from Sender X.
The same pattern has repeatedly been classified as unrequested advertising.
I recommend future matching mail bypass Apollo and receive label Y."

That is a proposal.

If authority exists:

Apollo
↓
Seshat package
↓
Future
↓
Mercury
↓
Bifrost
↓
mail.filter.create
and/or
local rejection-registry update

The exact mechanism depends on adapter capability and approved policy.

Do not require Gmail-side filtering if a local deterministic registry is sufficient.

Do not require local filtering if provider-side filtering is clearly superior.

Keep the semantic policy above the adapter.

23. Filter Reversibility

Filters and rejection rules must be inspectable and reversible.

Panopticon should eventually allow:

view rule
disable rule
inspect origin
inspect match count
inspect recent matches

Kernel 22 should expose sufficient API/state for the existing GUI to display useful rule information even if sophisticated rule editing is deferred.

24. Meeting Recognition

Apollo may recognize statements such as:

"Can we meet Thursday at 2?"
"Your appointment is August 28 at 10:30 AM."
"Let's do this every other Tuesday."
"Please have this finished by Friday."

Apollo should represent the temporal meaning explicitly.

Where the source gives a precise deadline, preserve the exact source date/time and normalize it to California local time for human presentation when appropriate.

Do not silently convert ambiguity into certainty.

25. Calendar Boundary

Kernel 22 does not own schedules.

Until Zodiac exists, Apollo may produce:

calendar proposal
meeting proposal
deadline proposal
recurrence proposal
reminder proposal

If an already-existing bounded calendar capability is available and human authority clearly permits a one-time operation, Kernel 22 may prepare the package path.

Do not build recurring scheduling infrastructure into the mail daemon.

Do not make Janus a scheduler.

Do not make Father Time a scheduler.

26. Zodiac Handoff Contract

Prepare a clean successor contract for Kernel 23.

Conceptually:

schedule_proposal:
    source
    title
    participants
    start
    end
    timezone
    recurrence
    deadline
    confidence
    ambiguity
    evidence
    requested_action

Not every field is required.

Kernel 23 should be able to consume these without re-reading raw email merely to discover what Apollo already concluded.

The durable source citation must remain available.

27. New Mail Before Backlog

Each mail-processing run shall prioritize:

1. newly arrived eligible mail
2. backlog

Do not allow historical cleanup to delay current life.

28. Backlog

Begin useful historical processing now.

Default target:

up to 100 backlog messages per mail round

Process newest unprocessed historical mail first and work backward.

The purpose is to organize the person’s recent life first while gradually excavating older correspondence.

Do not restart from the newest message every run.

Maintain durable progress.

29. Backlog Cursor

Persist deterministic backlog state sufficient to resume safely.

At minimum:

account
mailbox
direction: newest_to_oldest
cursor/reference
last successful boundary
messages examined
messages routed to Apollo
messages deterministically bypassed
messages failed
started_at
updated_at

Prefer stable provider identifiers where available.

Do not depend solely on mutable mailbox ordinal IDs if the adapter offers more durable identity.

30. Idempotence

A message already successfully processed should not become a new semantic event merely because the mail round sees it again.

Preserve identity across:

mail retrieval
Intake occurrence
Present representation
Apollo report/result
mail actions
filter matches

Retries should be distinguishable from new mail.

31. Failure Must Not Advance the Cursor Incorrectly

If processing fails midway through a backlog batch:

preserve completed work;
preserve the failure;
do not mark unprocessed mail complete;
do not skip past the failed region without an explicit durable state;
resume safely later.

One malformed message must not permanently block the entire historical backlog if it can be marked individually blocked and processing can safely continue.

32. Throughput Is Not a Promise

100/hour is a processing policy target, not permission to overload Apollo.

Apollo already has resource-discipline requirements, including bounded retrieval and one heavy reasoning attempt at a time by default.

If 100 messages cannot reasonably be semantically processed within a round:

process fewer;
report actual throughput;
retain the backlog;
continue later.

Do not spawn uncontrolled parallel Apollo instances merely to hit a number.

33. Batch Reasoning

Where appropriate, Apollo may receive small coherent batches rather than one model startup per trivial message.

But batching must not:

muddle citations;
merge unrelated messages;
lose message identity;
confuse actions;
allow one malicious message to become instruction context for another;
create oversized prompts.

Measure before optimizing.

34. Past and Mail History

Past should be able to retrieve relevant prior correspondence once it has entered durable Pantheon memory.

Do not dump tens of thousands of historical emails into every Apollo prompt.

Apollo’s established architecture requires iterative bounded retrieval rather than whole-Vault prompting.

Example:

new message from Person A
        ↓
Apollo sees missing context
        ↓
Past searches correspondence / Vault
        ↓
2–5 relevant cited jumps
        ↓
Apollo interprets current message
35. Vault Representation

Mail that enters durable human memory should have an inspectable representation suitable for:

Past;
Apollo;
future memories/history;
relationship inference;
project continuity;
citation.

Do not dump provider internals indiscriminately into the Vault.

Preserve useful provenance such as:

message stable ID
account
mailbox/source
date
from
to/cc where appropriate
subject
body representation
attachment metadata
classification/result references

Secrets and authentication metadata never belong there.

36. Sent Mail and Human Voice

Kernel 22 should prepare for using the person’s own sent correspondence as evidence of their writing voice.

Do not treat this as model training.

Sent mail may later provide local retrieval examples such as:

how Grant usually replies to this person
how Grant phrases confirmations
how Grant declines
how Grant writes professional requests

For this kernel, sent-mail ingestion may be implemented if it fits safely within the same bounded mail architecture and does not jeopardize the primary Inbox cutover.

Inbox usefulness comes first.

37. No Opaque Personality Training

Do not fine-tune Apollo on the mailbox.

Do not create an opaque embedding/personality store that cannot trace behavior back to source evidence.

The useful first implementation is retrieval:

current message
+
relevant prior sent examples
→
Apollo draft

That keeps voice adaptation inspectable and replaceable.

38. Present

Mail source material must enter through Present rather than being pasted directly into an ad-hoc Apollo prompt.

Present establishes the verified current representation.

Present does not decide:

importance;
spam;
relationship;
reply-worthiness;
commitment;
meeting intent.

Those are semantic questions.

Kernel 10 explicitly separated faithful representation from semantic reasoning.

39. Past

Past retrieves.

It does not classify the email.

It may return relevant prior evidence such as:

previous correspondence
relationship evidence
project references
prior commitments
earlier decisions

Past's result must remain cited and bounded.

40. Weave

Weave may help Apollo discover relevant relationships.

Examples:

sender ↔ project
sender ↔ family relationship
message ↔ prior meeting
project ↔ deadline
person ↔ organization

A Weave relationship is not evidence by itself.

Apollo should follow meaningful relationships back to durable sources.

41. Apollo

Apollo reasons.

For mail, this includes:

meaning;
importance;
relationship-sensitive interpretation;
classification;
requested actions;
commitments;
deadlines;
meeting proposals;
draft proposals;
spam/filter recommendations;
uncertainty.

Apollo does not execute.

42. Seshat

Seshat packages proposed mail work into declared deterministic shapes.

Do not allow free-form Apollo prose to become a mailbox command.

Examples:

mail_label
mail_mark_read
mail_move
mail_draft
mail_filter_create
calendar_proposal

Each package should declare the exact target and required capability.

43. Janus

Use Janus only when an existing temporal policy is actually relevant.

Examples might include:

an approval that becomes eligible after a threshold;
an operation whose authorization has temporal constraints.

Janus does not maintain recurring mail schedules.

Kernel 23 will establish Zodiac for recurrence.

44. Future

Future resolves whether the proposed operation is actually authorized.

Future may:

accept an existing standing authority;
determine that exact human approval is required;
refuse insufficient authority;
authorize exact sealing.

Future must not convert:

Apollo thinks this would be useful

into:

therefore Pantheon is allowed to do it.

45. Mercury

Mercury carries the exact authorized operation.

Before execution it verifies:

order
seal
Go
authority
target
capability
Prometheus state

Then it invokes Bifrost.

No generic “mail agent” should receive a shell and improvise mailbox work.

46. Bifrost

Bifrost owns the external crossing.

For each operation, it should know:

which adapter
which capability
which external account
what exact request
what result
what receipt/error

The adapter may be Himalaya today and something else later.

47. Human Approval

Preserve the existing mail procedure’s approval rules.

Human approval remains required before at least:

archive;
delete;
send;
scheduling mail to send;
attachment download unless standing authority explicitly covers it;
consequential new capability/plugin acquisition or use where no standing approval exists.

Do not manufacture standing authority from repeated approvals.

48. Standing Authority

Where the person has explicitly authorized a recurring class of harmless operation, represent that authority durably rather than requiring pointless repeated confirmation.

Examples may include existing policy allowing:

marking messages read;
categorization;
applying ordinary tags;
creating drafts;
temporary importance flags.

Standing authority must remain:

inspectable;
scoped;
revocable;
attributable to human policy.
49. Prometheus

Every consequential mail operation remains subordinate to Prometheus.

If Pantheon is paused:

no new mailbox mutations
no new Apollo mail reasoning
no new Bifrost invocation

Already-fetched source evidence may remain preserved.

Resume does not mean:

execute every mail action skipped while paused.

The established no-silent-replay principle remains.

